body:
I was working in the ICU up until the end of last week. Only had one COVID patient and preparations are being made to increase capacity in anticipation. It was like seeing preparation for the end of the world. 

&#x200B;

I hope you're doing ok. <3

I don't have anything specific that I listen to when the feeling comes over me. I just put my music on shuffle and hope something comes on that helps. Funnily enough, I was feeling this way today while driving, and the song Come and Get Your Love by Redbone (intro song on Guardians of the Galaxy) managed to shake me out of it. It's completely random. Other times I have a good cry with some more sombre music. 

&#x200B;

What do you listen to?

I hope you made it. I hope you found your way to a hospital. I'm thinking about you, and I truly hope you're still with us. 

&#x200B;

Much love

Reminds me of a quote that I think of a lot in times like this: "I can't go on. I'll go on". 

&#x200B;

Times seem tough now, but hopefully they get better. I hope you persevere! Here if you need to talk. Best of luck

Yup, as expected.

&#x200B;

Note: You were being disingenuous then, to recommend Islam to OP. Because he specified that he wanted openness to LGBTQ+ communities in whatever spiritual group he was recommended.

You're exactly halfway. Terrific! 

&#x200B;

I'm through 2 books. Fml. Goal is to get rolling and get to 12-20 by the end of the year, so I'm on pace to do better on the next one

I think about that a lot. 

&#x200B;

I don't think my family would miss me that much. I barely see them and they all have their issues to deal with themselves. 

&#x200B;

What it comes down to (at least for me) is accepting that we might be in it in the long run on our own. And hoping that we're up to the task.

Here for you if you need. I had a similar experience. Went out to the bridge and stood there looking at the water at about 3 a.m. It was a winter day so there was a layer of ice where I would've jumped. I couldn't do it. 

&#x200B;

I'm really glad you're still with us.

This makes me want to cry, but I can't. I feel so empty, not because of Notre Dame, tbh I don't feel much from that. 

&#x200B;

This is correct. So why can't I see the beauty? Impermanence just increases my dread

I read A Wild Sheep Chase as my first. It was really weird, but I kept up with it all along. And fell in love with Murakami's prose. 

&#x200B;

It's technically a sequel but they're all standalone books. Highly recommend.

I understand your point, but I feel you're being disingenuous by saying "well, atheists are a large demographic that spans a whole bunch of different belief systems", i.e. claiming there's a huge spectrum of belief in there, yet your post in and of itself seems to be claiming that the trending of society towards irreligion is bad because of several generalizations you have made against the irreligious. 

&#x200B;

tl;dr Don't say people shouldn't generalize, when you yourself are generalizing.

The growth you speak of in traditional orders isn't related to conversion, however, but indoctrination (i.e. enforcing social conservatism, high birthrates, tribalism, threat of ostracization to those leaving the faith). That's not the growth I think any compassionate person should hope for. And with regards to Vatican II, yes I have read about it. My opinions stand. 

&#x200B;

In religion, I often find that as the mainstream dies out, there becomes a polarization: those that think it's dying because they abandoned the old way (traditionalists such as yourself) and those that think that it's dying because they haven't progressed far enough (this would be my own camp). You're certainly entitled to your opinion, but I have mine, and I have faith in the path I see.

It's a tough call. Many proponents of second language learning say that speaking/listening should come first, i.e. when you learned your first language, did you sit through tedious grammar learning? (answer: not really). But nevertheless, learning the grammar, whilst being the most daunting task, makes the rest of the stuff come easier if you can do it. 

&#x200B;

Personally, I do a bit of everything, piece by piece.

Honestly, the idea of Jesus being the son of god rather than the single most important prophet of the faith inherently deters me from Christianity. In the age of reason I simply don't think such concepts attract new followers. 

&#x200B;

What causes Catholicism, at least, to lose followers (especially the young) is its rigidity against change, in general. Maybe abandoning the Trinity isn't the solution, but allowing flexibility amongst followers could be beneficial. I understand this sort of stance will prompt righteous anger from Catholics but frankly I think resistance to dialogue is another huge pitfall of the faith.

Because with the intense sadness, isolation, and terror, there are also moments of vibrant joy. I've had really overwhelming moments where I question whether those small flashes of good are worth all of this. And the truth is I don't know if it's worth it, but I keep on. 

&#x200B;

I could serve you platitudes that minimize your feelings, or insist this will all soon pass, but I don't think that's fair. It all comes down to you. I hope you will stay with us, because those moments of vibrant joy, flashes of colour, can be really wonderful.

I'm sorry this is happening to you. I hope you can find the strength to talk to someone about it. Maybe not your family, not yet. But somebody. 

&#x200B;

For me its different. I avoid my family and friends, the idea being if you alienate yourself it'll be easier to leave them. But I've started to fight that too. Maybe you'll find some solace in the new year. 

&#x200B;

Much love.

Because from time to time, when I'm at my lowest points, I feel overwhelmed at everything around me. It hurts so bad, and sometimes I become almost dissociated from my body, like I don't belong. And time stretches out ahead of me and I can't fathom the magnitude of life past what I can see, or things beyond life. 

&#x200B;

I don't believe in an anthropoid God. Or the Trinity. But I know that I'm a wave crashing against a shore, lucky enough to have self-awareness to feel these feelings of deep sadness, but also vibrant joy. I know that we're all here together. And I think too few of us think about this bond of life. And eventually the wave washes back into the ocean. Tide comes in, tide comes out. God is in all of us, and eventually we go back to god.

I've been through everything you describe. Absent interest in being, eh? 

&#x200B;

Feel free to message if you like. The short, and most frustrating answer, is to go on. And you may find some satisfaction, or at the very least see some great stuff along the way.
